> On Wednesday, September 10, 2014 12:40:33 Gleki Arxokuna wrote:
> > {ko catke la'e li 911} or {ko catke me'o 911}?
>
> Neither. I'm leaning toward "me'o", but "catke" is wrong. There appears to
> be
> a concept missing from the gimste, so I'm going to put "co'e" in its place.
> "ko judryco'e me'o 911".
>
> The missing concept includes opening a file, accessing a webpage, opening a
> cupboard, opening a book, or accessing a memory location. Opening a
> cupboard
> is "kargau", but that's what you do to the door; I'm talking about what
> you do
> to the cupboard. Any suggestions?
>

Isn't it what {kalri} is for? allowing, providing access for person x3 to
object x2

lo vreji cu kalri lo vasru be vo'a mi - the file gives me access to its
content. "give" is a terrible metaphor here, of course. a better
translation: "the file is open to me for providing access to its content".

i si'a lo zmiku vorme cu kalri fi mi lo drata kumfa i ku'i na kalri fi do i
e'u do cpacu lo jaspu be fo lo nu do te kalri dy.ky.



